From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] frontend: the Ftq's four queues as register-file modules

Ftq keeps its predictions and the BPU's redirect, resolve and commit
metadata in four Reg(Vec(FtqSize, ...)) arrays, written once at the
BPU's pointer and read by index: 64 words of 56, 194, 564 and 10 bits.
Synthesised, that is 53 kbit of flops behind 64:1 read muxes inside the
Ftq module, a 93 k line partition and one of the longest of the core's
synthesis; placed, a dense block the legaliser has to spread.

Each is now a RegVecFile: a small module holding the same Reg(Vec) with
explicit write (wen, addr, data) and read (addr -> data) ports, named
(FtqEntryQueue, FtqMetaQueueRedirect, FtqMetaQueueResolve,
FtqMetaQueueCommit) so a physical flow can replace it by a register-file
macro from its module name while simulation keeps the behavioural
model. Semantics are unchanged: a write port is the caller's
`when(cond) { vec(idx) := value }`, in port order, and a read port is
`vec(idx)`. The perf queue, which the design says should not exist in
hardware and which is read whole, is left as it was.

Carried by the bazel-orfs CoreMark/Joule study; not upstreamed.
---
diff -ruN a/src/main/scala/utils/RegVecFile.scala b/src/main/scala/utils/RegVecFile.scala
--- a/src/main/scala/utils/RegVecFile.scala
+++ b/src/main/scala/utils/RegVecFile.scala
@@ -0,0 +1,76 @@
+package utils
+
+import chisel3._
+import chisel3.util._
+
+/** A Reg(Vec) with its ports made explicit.
+  *
+  * Storage and semantics are those of `Reg(Vec(numEntries, gen))` with
+  * writes by index and reads by index: a write port's `wen`, `addr`,
+  * `data` are the `when(cond) { vec(idx) := value }` of the caller, in
+  * port order, so a later port wins a same-address write the way a
+  * later `:=` does; a read port is `vec(idx)`. What changes is that the
+  * array is a module of its own, named `name`, so a physical flow can
+  * replace it by a register-file macro while simulation keeps this
+  * behavioural model.
+  *
+  * Drive every port once with `idle()` after instantiation; `read` and
+  * `write` then override under the caller's own `when` conditions.
+  */
+class RegVecFileReadPort(addrWidth: Int, dataWidth: Int) extends Bundle {
+  val addr = Input(UInt(addrWidth.W))
+  val data = Output(UInt(dataWidth.W))
+}
+
+class RegVecFileWritePort(addrWidth: Int, dataWidth: Int) extends Bundle {
+  val wen  = Input(Bool())
+  val addr = Input(UInt(addrWidth.W))
+  val data = Input(UInt(dataWidth.W))
+}
+
+class RegVecFile[T <: Data](gen: T, numEntries: Int, numRead: Int, numWrite: Int, name: String)
+    extends Module {
+  override def desiredName: String = name
+
+  val addrWidth: Int = log2Up(numEntries)
+  val dataWidth: Int = gen.getWidth
+
+  val io = IO(new Bundle {
+    val readPorts  = Vec(numRead, new RegVecFileReadPort(addrWidth, dataWidth))
+    val writePorts = Vec(numWrite, new RegVecFileWritePort(addrWidth, dataWidth))
+  })
+
+  private val mem = Reg(Vec(numEntries, UInt(dataWidth.W)))
+
+  io.writePorts.foreach { w =>
+    when(w.wen) {
+      mem(w.addr) := w.data
+    }
+  }
+  io.readPorts.foreach { r =>
+    r.data := mem(r.addr)
+  }
+
+  /** Every port driven, nothing written: the caller's connections override. */
+  def idle(): Unit = {
+    io.readPorts.foreach(_.addr := 0.U)
+    io.writePorts.foreach { w =>
+      w.wen  := false.B
+      w.addr := 0.U
+      w.data := 0.U
+    }
+  }
+
+  /** Read port `i` at `addr`, as `gen`. */
+  def read(i: Int, addr: UInt): T = {
+    io.readPorts(i).addr := addr
+    io.readPorts(i).data.asTypeOf(gen)
+  }
+
+  /** Write port `i`: `data` to `addr`, under the caller's `when`. */
+  def write(i: Int, addr: UInt, data: T): Unit = {
+    io.writePorts(i).wen  := true.B
+    io.writePorts(i).addr := addr
+    io.writePorts(i).data := data.asUInt
+  }
+}
